# Line endings are a correctness problem in this repo, not a style preference.
#
# Every Windows machine here runs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this
# file Git rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. A Bash script checked out that way
# fails on its own second line under WSL's bash and under any other strict
# bash, with the misleading error:
#
#     : invalid option name line 42: set: pipefail
#
# That is what broke `bin/ai-adopt-globals` on t16 on 2026-08-27. The scripts
# themselves were fine; the checkout was not. Pin the endings by content type
# so the shell always receives LF no matter which machine cloned the repo.

# Let Git decide for anything not named below, and normalise to LF in history.
* text=auto

# Anything the shell executes must keep LF. `bin/` and `tests/` are listed as
# whole trees because most of their executables are deliberately
# extensionless, so a `*.sh` rule alone would miss them.
*.sh          text eol=lf
bin/**        text eol=lf
tests/**      text eol=lf
mcp/**        text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts keep CRLF. These rules come last on purpose: a later
# match wins, so `.ps1` and `.cmd` files inside `bin/` are not caught by the
# `bin/**` rule above.
*.ps1         text eol=crlf
*.bat         text eol=crlf
*.cmd         text eol=crlf

# Never touch binary content.
*.png         binary
*.jpg         binary
*.gif         binary
*.ico         binary
*.pdf         binary
*.zip         binary
*.gz          binary
